{"name":"Haint Blue","hex":"#c1d8d9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"haint blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pale shade of blue or green traditionally used to paint porch ceilings in the southern United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haint_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Haint blue","blocks":[{"text":"Haint blue is a collection of pale shades of blue-green that are traditionally used to paint porch ceilings in the Southern United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Haint_blue_Victorian_porch_ceiling.jpg/330px-Haint_blue_Victorian_porch_ceiling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haint_blue","wikidata":"Q52428980"},{"title":"Ghost","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, a ghost is the soul or spirit of a dead person or non-human animal that is believed by some people to be able to appear to the living. In ghostlore, descriptions of ghosts vary widely, from an invisible presence to translucent or barely visible wispy shapes to realistic, lifelike forms, whether they resemble humans or animals. The deliberate attempt to contact the spirit of a deceased person is known as necromancy, or in spiritism as a séance. Other terms associated with it are apparition, haunt, haint, phantom, poltergeist, shade, specter, spirit, spook, wraith, demon, and ghoul."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/02/Hammersmith_Ghost.PNG/330px-Hammersmith_Ghost.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost","wikidata":"Q45529"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg/500px-NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg/500px-NAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relief from a carved funerary lekythos at Athens showing Hermes as psychopomp conducting the soul of the deceased, Myrrhine into Hades (c. 430–420 BC)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANAMA_Herm%C3%A8s_%26_Myrrhin%C3%A8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg/250px-Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Suushi_Yurei.jpg/250px-Suushi_Yurei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yūrei (Japanese ghost) from the Hyakkai Zukan, c. 1737","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASuushi_Yurei.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu